A||||6||VISCERAL||tach filter?
Z||000000||VISCERAL||07-11-99||11:36 AM||||Where and what is this devious tach filter located and where to get them? G.M.?<br>
Z||000001||GREENDANO||07-11-99||01:47 PM||||The tack filter is the little silver cylinder that's mounted by the ignition coil bracket.  Best place to find a new one is at a junk yard.  I got mine off a Lumina with the new style connector also to replace the bad filter with the typical melted connector I had.<br>  Dan-O
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||07-11-99||03:30 PM||||Be aware though, that GM's official replacement part is *not* doing its job properly because it is different than the original. I found out the hard way when developing our digital dashboard. In ordinary analog gauges it will probably result in incorrect RPM readings at higher RPMs. I now make my own tachfilters, which is very easy if you know how to solder a bit.<br>
Z||000003||Cooter||07-11-99||05:01 PM||||After reading Cliff's response, I now have a question. Can a failing tach filter cause the engine to misfire at higher (4000+) rpms and run fine at lower speeds??
Z||000004||theogre||07-11-99||05:32 PM||||Yes the filter could cause that. So could the stock coil, It's just not built well. Try running w/o the filter. If the problem persists then the coil, or something else, is your problem.<p>MSD,  <A HREF="http://www.msdignition.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.msdignition.com/</A>  and Moroso,  <A HREF="http://www.moroso.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.moroso.com/</A> , both make things to fit all GM cars. They are both excelent products. Many of their products are trully plug and play. No cutting anything required.<p>
Z||000005||Cooter||07-11-99||11:07 PM||||I already have a MSD 6AL and Msd GM replacement coil. This problem with the high speed miss comes and goes and doesn't set a code and has been very annoying! I will try running without the tach filter and see what happens.<p>Hey Cliff- How do I make one of your replacement tach filters???? I am pretty handy with the soldering iron, and mig welder and even Bondo if that would help!
Z||000006||JetroGT||07-14-99||09:10 PM||||I have found a replacememt for you. Mr. Rodney Dickman sells a replacement that works just like the original, but it looks different. I copied his ad for you. <br>http://www.exepc.com/~rodney1/<p>"GM is out of stock on tach filters.  This is the round "can" in front of<br>   the coil of the V6 and elsewhere on the L4.  If your tach does not<br>    work or acts funny, this may be the problem.  Easy to install and<br>     requires no cutting or splicing.  Plugs directly into the factory<br>  harness.  Not an exact replica but it works the same as the original."<p>              Fiero Engine Tach Filter (V6 or L4):<br>                  Price: $17.50 ($1.00 S&H)<br>              Note: Electrical part. Not returnable
